    <p>A liquid you can run across, punch without splashing, and then watch your hand sink into. Welcome to the strange world of fluids that break Newton's rule.</p>
  </div>
</header>

<main class="wrap">

<section>
  <h2>1. First, the "normal" rule</h2>
  <p class="lead">Water, oil and honey are <b>Newtonian fluids</b>: their <b>viscosity</b> (thickness, or resistance to flow) stays the same no matter how hard or fast you stir them. Push twice as hard and they flow twice as fast. Simple.</p>
  <div class="card">
    <p style="margin:0">A <b>non-Newtonian fluid</b> breaks this rule: <b>its viscosity changes depending on the force or speed you apply</b>. Hit it, shake it or squeeze it, and it suddenly becomes thicker — or runnier.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>2. Try it: oobleck</h2>
  <p class="lead">Oobleck is cornstarch mixed with water. Drop the ball gently and it sinks. Throw it hard and the surface turns solid for a moment.</p>
  <div class="card">
    <div class="tank" id="tank">
      <div class="surface"></div>
      <div id="ball" style="top: 199px;"></div>
      <div id="msg">🐢 Slow push → it flows like a liquid. Sinking…</div>
    </div>
    <div class="controls">
      <button id="slow" class="alt">Place gently 🐢</button>
      <button id="fast">Throw hard 💥</button>
    </div>
    <p class="note">Simplified animation for illustration.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>3. Why does it happen?</h2>
  <div class="grid2">
    <div class="ex"><div class="em">🐢</div><h3>Slow push</h3><p>Cornstarch particles float in water. Water acts like a lubricant between them, so they slide past each other easily — the mix flows like a liquid.</p></div>
    <div class="ex"><div class="em">💥</div><h3>Sudden hit</h3><p>The particles are pushed together too fast for water to flow out of the way. They jam into a tight network that pushes back — briefly acting like a solid.</p></div>
    <div class="ex"><div class="em">🔄</div><h3>Let go</h3><p>Once the force stops, water flows back between the particles, they unjam, and the "solid" melts back into liquid.</p></div>
  </div>
  <p class="note" style="margin-top:16px">Scientists call this jamming <b>shear thickening</b>. Other non-Newtonian fluids work in different ways — see below.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>4. The graph that shows it</h2>
  <p class="lead">Plot the force needed (shear stress) against how fast the fluid flows (shear rate). Newtonian fluids give a straight line. Non-Newtonian fluids bend.</p>
  <div class="card">
    <canvas id="chart" width="800" height="440"></canvas>
    <p class="note" style="margin-bottom:0">
      <span style="color:var(--water);font-weight:600">━ Newtonian</span> (water) ·
      <span style="color:var(--thick);font-weight:600">━ Shear-thickening</span> (oobleck) ·
      <span style="color:var(--thin);font-weight:600">━ Shear-thinning</span> (ketchup) ·
      <span style="color:#1d5bb0;font-weight:600">━ Bingham plastic</span> (toothpaste)
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>5. Four kinds of non-Newtonian fluids</h2>
  <div class="grid2">
    <div class="ex"><span class="chip c-thick">Shear-thickening</span><h3>Gets thicker when hit</h3><p>Oobleck, wet sand at the beach, some body armor gels. More force = more resistance.</p></div>
    <div class="ex"><span class="chip c-thin">Shear-thinning</span><h3>Gets runnier when moved</h3><p>Ketchup, paint, blood, shampoo. Shake the ketchup bottle and it suddenly pours. Paint spreads easily but does not drip.</p></div>
    <div class="ex"><span class="chip c-yield">Bingham plastic</span><h3>Needs a push to start</h3><p>Toothpaste, mayonnaise. Stays put like a solid until you squeeze hard enough, then flows.</p></div>
    <div class="ex"><span class="chip c-time">Thixotropic</span><h3>Thins over time</h3><p>Yogurt, some paints and clays. The longer you stir, the thinner it gets; left alone, it thickens again.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>6. Newtonian vs non-Newtonian</h2>
  <table>
    <tbody><tr><th></th><th>Newtonian</th><th>Non-Newtonian</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Viscosity</td><td>Constant</td><td>Changes with force, speed or time</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Graph</td><td>Straight line</td><td>Curve</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Punch it</td><td>Splashes</td><td>Oobleck: feels like hitting a wall</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Examples</td><td>Water, air, oil, honey</td><td>Oobleck, ketchup, blood, toothpaste, slime, quicksand</td></tr>
  </tbody></table>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>7. Make it at home</h2>
  <div class="card">
    <ol class="recipe">
      <li>Put <b>2 parts cornstarch</b> in a bowl.</li>
      <li>Slowly mix in <b>1 part water</b>, stirring gently (add food coloring if you like).</li>
      <li>Aim for a mix that drips slowly when you lift it, but cracks if you tap it fast.</li>
      <li>Try it: punch it, roll it into a ball, then open your hand and watch it melt.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="note" style="margin-bottom:0">Tip: don't pour oobleck down the sink — it can clog pipes. Throw it in the trash.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>8. Where it's used</h2>
  <div class="grid2">
    <div class="ex"><div class="em">🛡️</div><h3>Protective gear</h3><p>Shear-thickening gels in sports pads and experimental body armor stay soft until an impact hits.</p></div>
    <div class="ex"><div class="em">🎨</div><h3>Paint</h3><p>Shear-thinning paint glides off the brush but stays put on the wall without dripping.</p></div>
    <div class="ex"><div class="em">🩸</div><h3>Your blood</h3><p>Blood thins as it flows faster, which helps it move through tiny vessels.</p></div>
    <div class="ex"><div class="em">🏖️</div><h3>Quicksand</h3><p>Struggling fast makes it grip harder. Moving slowly lets you escape.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>

